## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical monocular 6-DoF pose estimator that handles symmetry, occlusion, and online adaptation in a real-world manufacturing setting. Emphasizes differentiable refinement, data strategies, and latency planning.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for pose refinement
- Symmetry handling and occlusion robustness
- Synthetic data + domain randomization
- Online adaptation with few-shot updates
- Real-time latency targets (embedded GPU)

## Code Example
```javascript
class PoseHead {
  constructor(featDim) {
    this.fcRot = linear(featDim, 4); // quaternion
    this.fcTrans = linear(featDim, 3);
  }
  forward(feat) {
    const q = normalize(this.fcRot(feat), 2);
    const t = this.fcTrans(feat);
    return { quat: q, trans: t };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate symmetry handling for objects with known symmetry? 
- How would you mitigate catastrophic forgetting during online adaptation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine learned features with classical cues under tight on-device latency, in a realistic retail robotics setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight CNN + classical feature fusion
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-5 shots)
- Latency budgeting (<=50 ms/frame)
- Robust evaluation: per-item accuracy, misorientation metrics, throughput

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch of forward pass
function forward(frame) {
  const rois = detectROIs(frame);
  for (const roi of rois) {
    const cnnFeat = smallCNN(roi);
    const cues = extractCues(roi); // hist, edges, texture
    const fused = fuse(cnnFeat, cues);
    const label = orientationHead(fused);
    if (label === 'misoriented') alert(roi);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle label drift with new item designs?
- What ablation studies would you run to verify cue contributions?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses capability to design edge-friendly 3D pose systems for dynamic outdoor scenes, handling wind-induced blur, occlusion, and rapid adaptation to new bolt geometries with minimal on-device training.

## Key Concepts

- Edge latency and memory constraints on embedded GPUs
- Monocular 3D pose estimation (6-DoF) from moving cameras
- Few-shot on-device adaptation (2-shot)
- Synthetic-to-real transfer with domain randomization
- Robustness to motion blur and lighting variation

## Code Example

```javascript
function fineTuneAdapter(model, supportImages, supportLabels) {
  // tiny adapter fine-tune on-device
  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    const batch = getBatch(supportImages, supportLabels);
    const loss = model.computeLoss(batch);
    model.backward(loss);
  }
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure generalization to new turbine models or bolt types?
- What guardrails prevent false positives in flight-critical decisions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design for real-time monocular 6-DoF pose with few-shot adaptation on edge hardware, including data strategy and robust evaluation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Lightweight backbone plus specialized heads for detection, segmentation, and pose
- On-device few-shot adaptation via adapters or compact fine-tuning
- Synthetic+real data and domain randomization
- Occlusion robustness and reprojection-based losses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: simple pose reprojection loss sketch
function poseLoss(pred, gt, intr) {
  // pred: 6-DoF [rx,ry,rz,tx,ty,tz], gt: ground truth
  // project 3D corners to 2D and compare
  const pts3D = corners3D();
  const pts2D = project(pts3D, pred, intr);
  return meanSquaredError(pts2D, gt2D);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure adaptation effectiveness on unseen feature types with minimal labeling?
- What strategies mitigate drift when features are tiny or heavily occluded?
